{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string using dynamic programming.\n    The function is case-sensitive and includes spaces as characters.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the LPS for substring s[i...j]\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # All single characters are palindromes of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for substrings of length 2 to n\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                # If characters at ends match, they contribute 2 to the LPS length\n                # plus the LPS length of the inner substring s[i+1...j-1].\n                # If length is 2, the inner substring is empty, so dp[i+1][j-1] is 0.\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + (dp[i + 1][j - 1] if length > 2 else 0)\n            else:\n                # If characters at ends do not match, take the maximum of\n                # excluding s[i] (LPS of s[i+1...j]) or excluding s[j] (LPS of s[i...j-1]).\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i][j - 1], dp[i + 1][j])\n\n    # The result for the entire string s[0...n-1] is stored in dp[0][n-1]\n    return dp[0][n - 1]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "from dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import List\n\n@dataclass\nclass ArrayInput:\n    values: List[int]\n\n@dataclass\nclass Element:\n    index: int\n    value: int\n\n@dataclass\nclass BitonicSubsequence:\n    element_indices: List[int]\n    sum_value: int\n    def sum(self) -> int: return self.sum_value\n\n@dataclass\nclass Result:\n    sum: int\n\ndef compute_lis_sums(arr: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes for every index i the maximum sum of any strictly increasing subsequence ending at i.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return []\n    lis_sums = list(arr)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lis_sums[i] = max(lis_sums[i], lis_sums[j] + arr[i])\n    return lis_sums\n\ndef compute_lds_sums(arr: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes for every index i the maximum sum of any strictly decreasing subsequence starting at i.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return []\n    lds_sums = list(arr)\n    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lds_sums[i] = max(lds_sums[i], lds_sums[j] + arr[i])\n    return lds_sums\n\ndef max_sum(arr: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence for the given array.\n    A sequence is bitonic if it is first strictly increasing and then strictly decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0 # Or handle as per specific requirements, 0 is a reasonable default for no elements\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    lis_sums = compute_lis_sums(arr)\n    lds_sums = compute_lds_sums(arr)\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = float('-inf')\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A bitonic subsequence must have a peak element.\n        # The sum is lis_sum[i] + lds_sum[i] - arr[i]\n        # because arr[i] is counted in both LIS ending at i and LDS starting at i.\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, current_bitonic_sum)\n    \n    # If all elements are negative and only single-element bitonic sequences are possible,\n    # max_bitonic_sum might still be float('-inf') if arr was empty, but we handled that.\n    # If arr contains elements and max_bitonic_sum is still float('-inf'), it means the sums\n    # were so small they didn't even beat negative infinity, which implies an issue or\n    # an array of elements that doesn't allow any valid sequence (e.g. empty or only non-positive values\n    # where all sums are less than negative infinity, which is impossible for integers).\n    # Since single elements are valid bitonic sequences, max_bitonic_sum will be at least max(arr).\n    return int(max_bitonic_sum)"}
